WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.531 --> 00:00:02.297
- I went on America's Got
Talent and got kicked off

00:00:02.297 --> 00:00:03.200
the show.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.294
They told me I'd never
make it as a performer.

00:00:05.294 --> 00:00:08.096
And then I started doing
YouTube and then everything

00:00:08.096 --> 00:00:08.929
took off.

00:00:08.929 --> 00:00:11.918
And when your passion is
what you do for a living,

00:00:11.918 --> 00:00:14.611
it's very hard to not become a workaholic.

00:00:14.611 --> 00:00:16.866
If you're not happy with
the person you are today,

00:00:16.866 --> 00:00:19.772
I 100% believe that you can change.

00:00:19.772 --> 00:00:22.677
- She's an American
violinist, singer, songwriter,

00:00:22.677 --> 00:00:25.115
composer, and performing artist.

00:00:25.115 --> 00:00:28.355
Her music video, Crystalize,
was the eighth most watched

00:00:28.355 --> 00:00:30.000
video in 2012.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.434
As of July 2016 her YouTube
channel has over eight

00:00:33.434 --> 00:00:36.961
million subscribers and
over a billion views.

00:00:36.961 --> 00:00:39.418
She's Lindsay Sterling and
here's my take on her top

00:00:39.418 --> 00:00:41.134
10 rules to success.

00:00:41.134 --> 00:00:42.983
Rule number two is my personal
favorite and make sure

00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:44.945
to stick around all the way
to the end for some special

00:00:44.945 --> 00:00:46.388
bonus clips.

00:00:46.388 --> 00:00:48.277
And, as always, as Lindsay's
talking if she says something

00:00:48.277 --> 00:00:50.643
that really resonates with
you, please leave it in the

00:00:50.643 --> 00:00:52.808
comments below, put quotes
around it so other people

00:00:52.808 --> 00:00:55.052
can be inspired as well.

00:00:55.052 --> 00:00:55.885
Enjoy.

00:00:59.005 --> 00:01:01.588
(upbeat music)

00:01:09.254 --> 00:01:11.419
- I think, honestly, being
true to yourself is the

00:01:11.419 --> 00:01:15.796
absolute key to being successful
in this kind of a model.

00:01:15.796 --> 00:01:19.912
Because you can't fake
authenticity, you can't fake

00:01:19.912 --> 00:01:21.688
loving something.

00:01:21.688 --> 00:01:25.619
And honestly, to any
artistic form or medium,

00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:29.786
you have to work so hard to
get your stuff heard or seen.

00:01:31.095 --> 00:01:34.747
And so if you don't absolutely
love it you will burn

00:01:34.747 --> 00:01:36.430
yourself out and the only
way I'm able to do what I do

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:40.250
is I'm almost a workaholic
because I love what I'm doing.

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:41.184
And it's okay.

00:01:41.184 --> 00:01:43.541
Because I'm enjoying it and
because I love the people

00:01:43.541 --> 00:01:46.861
that I work with, it doubles
as work and recreation

00:01:46.861 --> 00:01:48.832
and if I didn't love it
it would be impossible.

00:01:48.832 --> 00:01:50.884
I went on America's Got
Talent and got kicked off

00:01:50.884 --> 00:01:53.700
the show, they told me I'd
never make it as a performer.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:56.562
And then I started doing
YouTube and then everything

00:01:56.562 --> 00:01:57.395
took off.

00:01:57.395 --> 00:02:00.526
And so to be able, to be
invited back to one of the

00:02:00.526 --> 00:02:03.562
biggest shows in America
and a show that I got kicked

00:02:03.562 --> 00:02:06.590
off of, to be invited back, it
was a really cool experience

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:09.352
just because a lot of
people don't get the chance

00:02:09.352 --> 00:02:14.290
to redo a moment of their life
that was very devastating.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:16.110
And to be able to go back
on that stage and do it

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.536
the way I wanted to do it
and be able to give the

00:02:18.536 --> 00:02:21.035
performance I wanted,
it was really awesome.

00:02:21.035 --> 00:02:24.458
- There were also critics on the show,

00:02:24.458 --> 00:02:26.643
the judges were not always nice with you.

00:02:26.643 --> 00:02:29.223
Wasn't it like judgment
day where you felt like,

00:02:29.223 --> 00:02:32.698
"Ha ha, I'm back and
this time I'm a star."

00:02:32.698 --> 00:02:34.545
- You know, kind of, it was.

00:02:34.545 --> 00:02:37.342
I hope that my fans and
other people saw it that way,

00:02:37.342 --> 00:02:40.122
in the way of, you can't
let anyone else tell you

00:02:40.122 --> 00:02:42.462
who you are and how good you because,

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:45.620
everyone has their own opinions,
but I hope that they see,

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:47.744
"Wow the same way Lindsay
didn't listen to them

00:02:47.744 --> 00:02:49.756
"and she went back and was
able to prove them wrong,

00:02:49.756 --> 00:02:50.597
"I can do that.

00:02:50.597 --> 00:02:52.600
"If someone at school is
mean to me, it doesn't mean

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.681
"that I'm a bad person,
it doesn't mean I'm a dork

00:02:54.681 --> 00:02:58.126
"or a loser, I can become
whoever I want to be."

00:02:58.126 --> 00:03:00.382
And I hope that people see
that because that moment

00:03:00.382 --> 00:03:03.049
proved that to me and it's true.

00:03:04.736 --> 00:03:08.903
I wish, so when I first
started I wish I had realized

00:03:09.899 --> 00:03:13.737
that people were the most
important thing to me.

00:03:13.737 --> 00:03:16.807
Because there was a time,
you know, I'm such a hard

00:03:16.807 --> 00:03:20.400
worker and when your passion
is what you do for a living,

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.808
it's very hard to not become a workaholic.

00:03:23.808 --> 00:03:27.961
And there was a time where
I just almost lost sight of

00:03:27.961 --> 00:03:29.943
everything that was most
important to me because I was

00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:32.980
working so hard and I was
pushing so hard for these

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:35.904
dreams and these goals and
everything I wanted in life,

00:03:35.904 --> 00:03:40.082
and my life started to look
very gray and colorless

00:03:40.082 --> 00:03:43.626
and I realized I had lost a
lot of depth because I wasn't

00:03:43.626 --> 00:03:45.432
making time for those
most important things,

00:03:45.432 --> 00:03:47.181
which are my family.

00:03:47.181 --> 00:03:49.306
You know, the people in my
life or someone that maybe

00:03:49.306 --> 00:03:52.882
needed me at a time, but I
was too bust to even notice.

00:03:52.882 --> 00:03:55.186
And those are the things that you can't,

00:03:55.186 --> 00:03:57.218
you can't fulfill your full passion,

00:03:57.218 --> 00:03:59.291
whether it's through
music or art or dance,

00:03:59.291 --> 00:04:01.708
if you've lost sight of
the things that make you

00:04:01.708 --> 00:04:03.426
a real person.

00:04:03.426 --> 00:04:05.966
And I had to refind myself, almost, like,

00:04:05.966 --> 00:04:07.824
"Wow, what is it that makes me me?"

00:04:07.824 --> 00:04:09.949
And once I re-found that
it's like everything else

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:10.883
came together again.

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:13.696
I was able to write again,
I was able to create,

00:04:13.696 --> 00:04:16.836
but without those things,
then you lose sight of

00:04:16.836 --> 00:04:18.543
everything.

00:04:18.543 --> 00:04:20.839
I think like you said,
every single artist goes

00:04:20.839 --> 00:04:21.672
through that.

00:04:21.672 --> 00:04:23.997
And I think a lot of times
you think that the great

00:04:23.997 --> 00:04:27.044
writers are the people, they
just write hit after hit

00:04:27.044 --> 00:04:29.604
after hit, and they do
but it's also because they

00:04:29.604 --> 00:04:31.941
get through all the terrible songs first.

00:04:31.941 --> 00:04:33.700
And, I mean, I think
that's kind of every single

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:35.283
artist story.

00:04:35.283 --> 00:04:38.260
And, I mean, I love to
tell, when people ask,

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.698
"What's your best advice
for an upcoming musician,

00:04:40.698 --> 00:04:43.451
"or anybody?" I think it's
that you have to be able

00:04:43.451 --> 00:04:46.622
to rise form the failures
is what makes a successful

00:04:46.622 --> 00:04:47.455
person.

00:04:47.455 --> 00:04:49.283
'Cause everybody's going to have mishaps,

00:04:49.283 --> 00:04:51.567
they're going to fall down,
and it's the people that

00:04:51.567 --> 00:04:54.255
continuously pick themselves
up that are the ones

00:04:54.255 --> 00:04:56.789
that are going to find
their voice, make it,

00:04:56.789 --> 00:04:58.171
whatever you want to call it.

00:04:58.171 --> 00:05:00.214
- I love how for this
record you had fans actually

00:05:00.214 --> 00:05:02.193
do little videos that
talked about what they were

00:05:02.193 --> 00:05:03.912
brave enough to do in life.

00:05:03.912 --> 00:05:05.668
So for you, what was the
moment you realized you were

00:05:05.668 --> 00:05:07.589
brave enough to do this full time,

00:05:07.589 --> 00:05:10.170
just go right after your passion?

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:14.517
- Oh, I think it's once
I discovered YouTube.

00:05:14.517 --> 00:05:17.156
I mean, I had already been
going for it with full force

00:05:17.156 --> 00:05:21.759
but I was kind of wondering
if I had what it took

00:05:21.759 --> 00:05:24.066
to make it and I was really
discouraged at times 'cause

00:05:24.066 --> 00:05:25.965
everywhere I went I just kept being told,

00:05:25.965 --> 00:05:27.793
"No, you're not good enough."

00:05:27.793 --> 00:05:29.764
And that can be really
discouraging and so even though

00:05:29.764 --> 00:05:32.243
I was going for it, I was
kind of in the back of my

00:05:32.243 --> 00:05:34.265
mind just really scared.

00:05:34.265 --> 00:05:35.674
Like, "Am I going to
make it, I don't know."

00:05:35.674 --> 00:05:39.432
And then when I finally
discovered YouTube and I put up

00:05:39.432 --> 00:05:42.788
my first video, I just felt
so empowered and so excited

00:05:42.788 --> 00:05:45.847
because I was like, "Whoa,
finally something's working."

00:05:45.847 --> 00:05:49.005
And that's when I was like,
"I'm going to, I don't know how

00:05:49.005 --> 00:05:50.825
"this is going to work out,
but I'm going to chase this

00:05:50.825 --> 00:05:52.195
"with everything I've got."

00:05:52.195 --> 00:05:54.267
And that's when I was like, boom!

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:57.415
- And what about the
appreciation of hip hop and EDM,

00:05:57.415 --> 00:06:01.166
which both have played a role
in what you do musically?

00:06:01.166 --> 00:06:03.409
When did you first encounter
those and how did you

00:06:03.409 --> 00:06:04.909
come to love them?

00:06:06.854 --> 00:06:09.639
- I really fell in love
with EDM when I first heard,

00:06:09.639 --> 00:06:13.406
I always liked techno music,
I felt like it was kind of,

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:15.804
for lack of a better term,
it was great white girl

00:06:15.804 --> 00:06:17.338
dance music.

00:06:17.338 --> 00:06:18.993
I've always loved to dance and I was like,

00:06:18.993 --> 00:06:21.228
"Oh these beats, I can dance to this."

00:06:21.228 --> 00:06:24.276
It's not quite as, doesn't
need as much groove as

00:06:24.276 --> 00:06:25.961
hip hop did.

00:06:25.961 --> 00:06:27.843
So that's why I always
like EDM and then I really

00:06:27.843 --> 00:06:30.188
fell in love with it when
I discovered Skrillex,

00:06:30.188 --> 00:06:31.672
especially Bangarang.

00:06:31.672 --> 00:06:34.211
That song, I was like, "Oh
my gosh, I have to figure out

00:06:34.211 --> 00:06:38.011
"how I can pair the violin
with this weird dubstep stuff."

00:06:38.011 --> 00:06:41.688
'Cause it was so fun and
it made me want to move.

00:06:41.688 --> 00:06:43.183
And that was the beginning.

00:06:43.183 --> 00:06:46.940
You know, it never becomes,
I don't think it'll ever

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:49.602
be like, "Oh, I don't care,
I don't care anymore."

00:06:49.602 --> 00:06:52.111
Because I do care, it's
my art, it's my love,

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:53.034
it's my passion.

00:06:53.034 --> 00:06:55.330
And certain comments you're able to like,

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:58.340
"Oh whatever," but there's
some comments that get you

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.209
in the place that you're
a little bit vulnerable.

00:07:00.209 --> 00:07:01.042
- [Interviewer] That trigger you.

00:07:01.042 --> 00:07:02.656
- That trigger you, they
get you, you're like,

00:07:02.656 --> 00:07:04.180
"Don't talk about that,
that's actually something

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:07.706
"that I care about and I'm sensitive to."

00:07:07.706 --> 00:07:10.153
And I think it's so
important to realize that,

00:07:10.153 --> 00:07:13.586
especially in YouTube
there is so much good,

00:07:13.586 --> 00:07:16.338
but for some reason it's
easier to focus on the

00:07:16.338 --> 00:07:19.795
negative because it just,
it gets you so much more.

00:07:19.795 --> 00:07:21.225
- You'll get thousands
of positive comments and

00:07:21.225 --> 00:07:22.404
then 10 negative.

00:07:22.404 --> 00:07:24.761
- One negative, you're like, "Oh dang it."

00:07:24.761 --> 00:07:27.727
So it's so true that you
only really do have one

00:07:27.727 --> 00:07:30.238
stage in your mind that
you can really focus on

00:07:30.238 --> 00:07:32.595
at one time, and so the good, the bad,

00:07:32.595 --> 00:07:35.013
they're going to come and what
are you going to focus on?

00:07:35.013 --> 00:07:37.581
Am I going to sit and stew and respond,

00:07:37.581 --> 00:07:40.729
especially responding, if
you respond to the negative,

00:07:40.729 --> 00:07:42.550
that is a lot of times
the one that you want to

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:43.668
respond to.

00:07:43.668 --> 00:07:45.778
But it's like, "Or am I
going to feed the good."

00:07:45.778 --> 00:07:48.185
Am I going to feed these
people who keep coming back

00:07:48.185 --> 00:07:49.995
and telling me really nice things,

00:07:49.995 --> 00:07:52.526
that's where I should put my
energy, that's where I should

00:07:52.526 --> 00:07:55.501
put my thoughts and focus
and it does take a lot of,

00:07:55.501 --> 00:07:58.418
you know, concentration to do that.

00:07:59.484 --> 00:08:00.680
- [Interviewer] Yeah, awareness too.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:02.753
- Awareness, that's the
word I'm looking for.

00:08:02.753 --> 00:08:05.122
But I think that's the key
is just there's good and bad

00:08:05.122 --> 00:08:07.528
all around us, everywhere
and trying really hard to

00:08:07.528 --> 00:08:09.640
focus on the good and let that feed you.

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:12.568
You know, every tour I
like to try to step it up.

00:08:12.568 --> 00:08:14.611
I want my fans that have,
a lot of my fans come to

00:08:14.611 --> 00:08:17.625
multiple shows and so I always
want them to be surprised

00:08:17.625 --> 00:08:18.704
when they come back.

00:08:18.704 --> 00:08:20.860
I don't want it to feel like
the last Lindsay Sterling

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:21.693
show.

00:08:21.693 --> 00:08:24.088
And, you know, "Been there, done that."

00:08:24.088 --> 00:08:25.752
So I always try to do
something that I've never done

00:08:25.752 --> 00:08:29.432
before and this tour is a lot of fun.

00:08:29.432 --> 00:08:31.785
There's some new tricks I
have up my sleeve that I,

00:08:31.785 --> 00:08:34.714
you know, I worked really
hard to learn and to master

00:08:34.714 --> 00:08:38.412
these new skills, but anyways,
super excited about it.

00:08:38.412 --> 00:08:42.406
I think the courage to be
really open about some of

00:08:42.406 --> 00:08:44.162
the things I've been through
in the past all comes

00:08:44.162 --> 00:08:47.546
from experiencing those
things and when I was going

00:08:47.546 --> 00:08:50.112
through, say, when I was
going through some of these

00:08:50.112 --> 00:08:54.109
things I felt so alone and a
lot of girls, or people, do.

00:08:54.109 --> 00:08:56.161
You feel like nobody can
understand you because a lot of

00:08:56.161 --> 00:08:58.526
these problems deal
with isolating yourself

00:08:58.526 --> 00:08:59.786
and feeling alone.

00:08:59.786 --> 00:09:02.488
And one of the things that
really helped me have the

00:09:02.488 --> 00:09:06.076
courage to change was I
realized that I'm not alone.

00:09:06.076 --> 00:09:08.513
There's actually people who
understand me and there's

00:09:08.513 --> 00:09:11.155
people who have actually overcome this.

00:09:11.155 --> 00:09:13.785
And that gave me hope and
so that's why I really

00:09:13.785 --> 00:09:16.934
strive to give a positive
message of hope about, like,

00:09:16.934 --> 00:09:19.311
if you're not happy with
the person you are today,

00:09:19.311 --> 00:09:22.431
I 100% believe that you can change.

00:09:22.431 --> 00:09:25.182
So that's what I like to
hopefully share with young girls

00:09:25.182 --> 00:09:28.655
and I would say that's what
gave me the courage to share.

00:09:28.655 --> 00:09:30.953
I've always played the
violin ever since I was six

00:09:30.953 --> 00:09:34.000
years old I begged for lessons
and I've always played.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.815
And it was actually when I
was in college that I kind of

00:09:36.815 --> 00:09:39.496
just realized one day that
I didn't love it anymore

00:09:39.496 --> 00:09:40.757
and I used to love it.

00:09:40.757 --> 00:09:44.606
And I specifically didn't
study music in school because

00:09:44.606 --> 00:09:47.430
I didn't ever want to
have the love of my music

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:48.377
beat out of me.

00:09:48.377 --> 00:09:49.909
So it kind of broke my
heart when I was like,

00:09:49.909 --> 00:09:51.880
"I don't even enjoy this anymore."

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:55.366
And os I started thinking,
"What can I do to invigorate

00:09:55.366 --> 00:09:56.471
"my passion?"

00:09:56.471 --> 00:09:59.714
I started playing with bands,
I started anywhere I could

00:09:59.714 --> 00:10:02.480
go to kind of try out new
styles of music and I've

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:05.850
always loved electronic music
and it just hit me one day,

00:10:05.850 --> 00:10:08.154
"I need to play and write
the kind of music that I

00:10:08.154 --> 00:10:09.987
"am loving right now."

00:10:13.709 --> 00:10:16.423
And so I did and it just
brought it back to life for me,

00:10:16.423 --> 00:10:18.516
it made it so creative
and I became a creator,

00:10:18.516 --> 00:10:20.375
not just a musician
and that's I think what

00:10:20.375 --> 00:10:22.292
invigorated my passion.

00:10:25.088 --> 00:10:26.684
- Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:10:26.684 --> 00:10:29.569
I made this video because
John Thoren asked me to.

00:10:29.569 --> 00:10:31.206
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want

00:10:31.206 --> 00:10:33.287
me to profile next, leave
it down in the comments

00:10:33.287 --> 00:10:34.871
below and I'll see what I can do.

00:10:34.871 --> 00:10:36.741
I also love to know what
did Lindsay say that

00:10:36.741 --> 00:10:39.109
had the biggest impact on
you, which rule did you

00:10:39.109 --> 00:10:40.256
enjoy the most?

00:10:40.256 --> 00:10:43.401
What are you going from
her message to immediately

00:10:43.401 --> 00:10:44.757
apply to your life of business?

00:10:44.757 --> 00:10:46.693
Please leave it in the
comments and I'm going to join

00:10:46.693 --> 00:10:47.753
in the discussion.

00:10:47.753 --> 00:10:49.674
Finally, I want to take a
quick shout at Steph Reveron.

00:10:49.674 --> 00:10:52.559
Steph, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:10:52.559 --> 00:10:55.230
Your One Word, and sending
in that picture of you

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:57.363
at the cafe reading it, I
hope you're enjoying it and

00:10:57.363 --> 00:10:58.724
getting a lot of value from it.

00:10:58.724 --> 00:11:01.244
So thank you guys again for
watching, I believe in you,

00:11:01.244 --> 00:11:03.499
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself and

00:11:03.499 --> 00:11:05.388
whatever your one word is.

00:11:05.388 --> 00:11:07.805
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:11:14.009 --> 00:11:16.055
- It's the same way kids
look up to Taylor Swift

00:11:16.055 --> 00:11:18.946
and they want to get a little
guitar and be Taylor Swift.

00:11:18.946 --> 00:11:21.081
My parents used to take me
to orchestra concerts and so

00:11:21.081 --> 00:11:23.083
that was my version of a rockstar.

00:11:23.083 --> 00:11:24.778
I was like, "Oh my gosh,
I have to get one of those

00:11:24.778 --> 00:11:26.274
"wooden things."

00:11:26.274 --> 00:11:29.209
But yeah, I played classical
violin all growing up

00:11:29.209 --> 00:11:31.862
and I started to just, I
had gotten burnt out and I

00:11:31.862 --> 00:11:34.563
wanted to love it again and
so I started experimenting

00:11:34.563 --> 00:11:37.751
with all different sorts of
music and I found out that

00:11:37.751 --> 00:11:40.941
I absolutely loved EDM and
electronic dance and dubstep

00:11:40.941 --> 00:11:45.045
music and nobody, no
record labels thought I was

00:11:45.045 --> 00:11:47.565
worthwhile or that my
project was marketable and so

00:11:47.565 --> 00:11:51.994
I started doing it myself
on YouTube and that's when

00:11:51.994 --> 00:11:55.395
it just kind of, "Whoa, I
guess it is marketable,"

00:11:55.395 --> 00:11:56.888
is what I kind of discovered.

00:11:56.888 --> 00:11:59.296
I guess I surround my crew, my tour crew,

00:11:59.296 --> 00:12:01.990
I surround myself with
really great people.

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:04.295
I have the best tour
crew and so they kind of,

00:12:04.295 --> 00:12:07.667
I mean honestly, just say
one day I wanted to be crazy

00:12:07.667 --> 00:12:10.735
and went out and got drunk,
they would all just be like,

00:12:10.735 --> 00:12:11.568
"Whoa."

00:12:11.568 --> 00:12:13.844
They would be so turned
off they'd just be like,

00:12:13.844 --> 00:12:14.831
"What are you doing?"

00:12:14.831 --> 00:12:16.232
They'd probably take the beer
out of my hand and be like,

00:12:16.232 --> 00:12:18.406
"Come here, Lindsay,
what's wrong with you?"

00:12:18.406 --> 00:12:20.174
Because they know me
so well and they just,

00:12:20.174 --> 00:12:22.215
they're there to protect
me and help me, as well.

00:12:22.215 --> 00:12:25.111
I wasn't like I have always
been a very optimistic upbeat

00:12:25.111 --> 00:12:26.256
person.

00:12:26.256 --> 00:12:29.590
When I first came to college,
it was a time that I was

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:32.607
trying to figure out, "Who
am I? What makes me special?"

00:12:32.607 --> 00:12:35.087
And I started to find most
of my value in the fact

00:12:35.087 --> 00:12:36.420
that I was thin.

00:12:37.485 --> 00:12:42.227
For several years I had no
idea I had become anorexic.

00:12:42.227 --> 00:12:44.482
And be places with people I cared about,

00:12:44.482 --> 00:12:47.359
but what I was thinking
about was the extra grease

00:12:47.359 --> 00:12:50.285
on the pizza or the
calories that I knew were in

00:12:50.285 --> 00:12:51.869
that shake.

00:12:51.869 --> 00:12:54.084
And I just was talking to
my mom one day and I broke

00:12:54.084 --> 00:12:57.465
down crying because I
realized for the first time

00:12:57.465 --> 00:13:00.545
that I had a problem and
that this isn't normal.

00:13:00.545 --> 00:13:02.373
People don't think like this.

00:13:02.373 --> 00:13:04.344
I finally realized I wasn't happy.

00:13:04.344 --> 00:13:07.189
Any time there's a change to
be made, you have to realize

00:13:07.189 --> 00:13:09.900
that there's somewhere
else you want to be.

00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:12.072
And I wanted, basically, to be happy.

00:13:12.072 --> 00:13:14.808
That was far more important
than being the skinniest

00:13:14.808 --> 00:13:16.058
girl in a room.

00:13:17.966 --> 00:13:20.822
Once I realized that there
was a problem that's when

00:13:20.822 --> 00:13:22.049
I could change.

00:13:22.049 --> 00:13:23.708
I knew that I could be happy again.

